<speak>
<p>Recall our original four by four circulant Hadamard matrix. Its first row is negative one, one, one, one. Each following row is a right cyclic shift. Our goal is to replace the matrix by one algebraic object that remembers all those shifts.<break time="700ms"/></p>
<p>Use the entries as polynomial coefficients. We get h of X equals negative one plus X plus X squared plus X cubed. Work with the rule X to the fourth equals one, so the powers wrap around. In dimension n, this is the integer group ring of the cyclic group: integer polynomials modulo X to the n minus one. Its unique representative of degree less than n records the row unambiguously.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>Now multiply h by X. The last term becomes X to the fourth, which is one. The coefficients become one, negative one, one, one. That is exactly a right cyclic shift. Multiply by X again for the next row. Row j corresponds to X to the j minus one times h. After n shifts, the cycle returns to h.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>To encode dot products we need another operation: star. Replace X by X inverse. This fixes the constant coefficient and reverses the other cyclic positions. With generic coefficients, a, b, c, d becomes a, d, c, b. Our actual row is unchanged because its final three entries agree. Thus, for this example, h star equals h. Star reverses positions; it does not negate the coefficients.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>Multiply h by h star in this group ring. The coefficient at each power X to the a is the dot product of the first row with its a step right shift. At the constant term we compare the row with itself: four squares of signs give four. At X we get negative one, negative one, one, one, whose sum is zero. The two step and three step dot products are also zero.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>So every nonconstant coefficient vanishes. The whole product is simply four. One equation has encoded every orthogonality condition of our example.<break time="700ms"/></p>
<p>The same dictionary works in every dimension, provided all n coefficients are positive or negative one. The matrix equation, H times H transpose equals n times the identity, is equivalent to h times h star equals n in the group ring. Forward, orthogonality makes the nonconstant coefficients zero, while the signs give constant coefficient n. Conversely, the polynomial identity makes the first row orthogonal to every nontrivial shift. Shift any pair of rows back together: their dot product becomes one of those same correlations. Every distinct pair is therefore orthogonal.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>The sign hypothesis is essential. In dimension four, the constant polynomial two also has product four with its star, but its coefficient row is two, zero, zero, zero. It gives twice the identity matrix, not a Hadamard matrix. With the sign hypothesis in place, the dictionary is exact. Explore the five smaller nodes for its ingredients. Next we use the identity to restrict the order n.<break time="1000ms"/></p>
</speak>
